We thank all reviewers for their thoughtful and constructive feedback. We have carefully addressed major and minor concerns, and the revised paper is substantially stronger as a result. In brief, we confirmed that the identified model units are neurally predictive of human VWFA brain recordings, and that our model behaviorally predicts which fonts are easier for individuals with dyslexia. Below we summarize the key updates and clarifications for the Area Chair’s consideration. 

&nbsp;

# Neural alignment of model units and brain recordings
Several reviewers requested quantitative evidence linking the model’s VWF-selective units to the human VWFA. With the recent release of Marvi et al. (2025), we were able to add a full neural alignment analysis (Fig. 5, page 7). Using a standard encoding-model framework, we show that VWF-selective units predict human VWFA responses significantly better than randomly sampled units. This provides direct biological validation of our localized units and addresses the reviewers’ core concerns regarding mechanistic realism. 

&nbsp;

# Behavioral validation via font-sensitivity experiments 
To further show our ablation captures clinically meaningful aspects of dyslexia, we added new experiments evaluating the model on dyslexia-relevant fonts (pages 9–10). The dyslexic model reproduces the elevated sensitivity to visually challenging fonts that is reported in dyslexic readers, while the intact model does not. Importantly, this also serves as a proof-of-concept demonstration of how the model could be used to evaluate or design fonts that reduce visual stress. 

&nbsp;

# Clearer articulation of novelty and relationship to prior work
We have strengthened the positioning of our contributions relative to AlKhamissi et al. (2025) and Du et al. (2025). Our work goes beyond prior studies by introducing a mechanistic framework for simulating a neurodevelopmental reading disorder inside a vision-language model. By identifying VWFA-analogous units with a neuroimaging-inspired localizer, causally perturbing them, and demonstrating both neural and behavioral specificity, including the two new validation analyses, our approach provides a new computational tool for understanding dyslexia. To the best of our knowledge, no prior work has modeled dyslexia (or other brain disorders) at this mechanistic level within a VLM. 

&nbsp;

# Improved transparency and reproducibility
Following reviewer suggestions, we have added full prompt descriptions and examples (Appendix A.3) and clarified computational resources (Appendix A.5).

&nbsp;

# Clarified modeling choices and limitations
Finally, we expanded discussion around biological realism, lesion modeling, and developmental considerations (page 10). While computational models inevitably simplify neural processes, our approach mirrors established VWFA-linked deficits and offers a uniquely controlled environment for testing causal hypotheses that are experimentally not easily accessible in human participants. 

&nbsp;


# Overall contribution and impact
With the addition of neural alignment results and behavioral font-sensitivity experiments, the paper now brings together convergent neural and behavioral evidence that the model’s impairments authentically parallel dyslexia. We believe the revised submission represents a well-validated, and novel step toward using large-scale models to study and potentially assist with neurodevelopmental reading disorders such as dyslexia. 

&nbsp;

We kindly ask the Area Chair to consider these substantial improvements and clarifications in the final decision process. For further details on minor improvements and responses to specific reviewer comments, we invite the Area Chair to consult the responses provided below.
